# REPAIR — the operations a planner may apply to broken code, and the evaluator that judges them.
#
# ⚠ WHY THE EVALUATOR IS A MICROFUNCTION AND NOT PYTHON, and why that is forced rather than stylistic.
#
# A repair has to be JUDGED before it is chosen, and the driver judges by imagining: it copies the graph
# onto a workbench and applies a candidate there. `dispatch.service` refuses an imagined target, so
# nothing inside a plan can reach the world — which means a candidate repair can NEVER be evaluated by
# running the patched code. Execution is not available where the choosing happens.
#
# So the evaluation must DERIVE the answer from the structure: read the operator, the threshold and the
# branch bodies, and work out what the function would return. That is pure graph reasoning, it runs
# happily on a copy, and it is exactly the discipline the previous engine's repair probe reached from the
# other direction ("derive by reasoning, never by exec; confirm by execution at the END").
#
# The division, stated once: IMAGINATION DERIVES, REALITY EXECUTES. The evaluator below decides which
# repair to adopt; running the emitted source afterwards is the independent gate that says the derivation
# was right about the real language.

# --- the repair operations ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two INDEPENDENT families, deliberately, so the search has a real choice rather than one dressed-up
# option — and so "which repair did it pick, and was the other also valid" is a question with an answer.

# Family 1 — the boundary is right, the strictness is wrong.
fn relax_comparison(c: comparison) -> comparison:
    SET F(c) "op" "ge"

# Family 2 — the strictness is right, the boundary is off by one.
fn lower_threshold(c: comparison) -> comparison:
    GET R(rhs) F(c) "right"
    ATTR R(v) R(rhs) "value"
    ADD R(v2) R(v) -1
    SET R(rhs) "value" R(v2)

# --- the evaluator ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ Models `gt` and `ge` ONLY, and REFUSES anything else: an unmodelled operator leaves `agrees` unset, so
# the goal stays unmet and no repair is credited with a success it did not earn. The reach membrane again.
#
# ⚠⚠ THE FIRST VERSION CLAIMED THAT REFUSAL IN A COMMENT AND DID NOT MAKE IT. It fell straight through to
# the `gt` path for every operator that was not `ge`, so `if age < 18` was derived as though it read
# `age > 18` — `classify(10)` came back `'minor'` when the code plainly returns `'adult'`. Confidently
# wrong, on code we cannot read, with nothing to indicate it. Found by testing the docstring's claim
# rather than the code's behaviour, which is the only way that class of bug surfaces.
#
# The guard is now FIRST and explicit, because a membrane described in prose is not a membrane.

fn evaluate_case(k: case) -> checked_case:
    GET R(f) F(k) "of"
    GET R(c) R(f) "condition"
    ATTR R(op) R(c) "op"

    # Refuse before deriving anything: is this an operator we actually model?
    EQ R(isge) R(op) "ge"
    JMPIF R(isge) .modelled
    EQ R(isgt) R(op) "gt"
    JMPIF R(isgt) .modelled
    SET F(k) "unmodelled_operator" R(op)
    JMP .done

.modelled:
    GET R(rhs) R(c) "right"
    ATTR R(thr) R(rhs) "value"
    ATTR R(in) F(k) "input"

    # Below the threshold, no operator we model takes the branch.
    LT R(lt) R(in) R(thr)
    JMPIF R(lt) .otherwise

    # Above it, every operator we model does.
    EQ R(eq) R(in) R(thr)
    JMPNOT R(eq) .then

    # ⭐ AT the threshold — the only input where `gt` and `ge` disagree, which is the whole bug.
    EQ R(isge) R(op) "ge"
    JMPIF R(isge) .then
    JMP .otherwise

.then:
    GET R(b) R(f) "then"
    JMP .read
.otherwise:
    GET R(b) R(f) "otherwise"

.read:
    GET_AT R(s) R(b) "stmt" 0
    GET R(rv) R(s) "value"
    ATTR R(out) R(rv) "value"
    SET F(k) "observed" R(out)
    ATTR R(exp) F(k) "expects"
    EQ R(ok) R(out) R(exp)
    SET F(k) "agrees" R(ok)

.done:
    CONST R(end) true
